  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nAs of early this afternoon, satellite shows expansive stratus \nover this FA. Pockets of drizzle, once prevalent this morning, \nhave been becoming spottier as the day wears on. Winds have also\nsubsided, as winds aloft to mix down are not nearly as strong \nas earlier today. Therefore, for the rest of today, expect gray,\ncloudy, and chilly conditions with a stiff but slowly subsiding\nnortherly wind. Zooming out, an upper low over the central US \nis propagating to the north and east this afternoon. Surface low\npressure, currently just north of Kansas City MO, will track to\nthe north and east overnight into Wednesday. This will set the \nstage for another round of rainfall tonight into Wednesday, \nalbeit with a further southeast track compared to yesterdays \nsystem, rain will be confined to southeastern ND and west \ncentral MN. Rain will end Wednesday night. One other hazard to \nlook out for the next few mornings is fog. Expansive cloud cover\nand lingering winds should keep fog coverage in check Wednesday\nmorning. The exception could be the Devils Lake Basin where \nclearing skies may sneak in, but predictability in this is low. \nThursday morning brings the potential for more widespread fog as\nthe low departs, taking lingering cloud cover with it. \n\nBehind this system, ridging will build in as we go into the \nweekend, slowly flattening over the weekend. This will leave us \ndry and warm for early November, with high temperatures in the \n40s to 50s. There are signs a system or two may try to ride the \ntop of the ridge, especially as we transition to northwest/zonal\nflow Sunday into next Monday. At this time, precipitation \ntotals appear meager at best. \n\n...Rain Tonight into Wednesday...\n\nAs the previously mentioned surface and upper low track to the \nnorth and east from the Central Plains this afternoon towards \nLake Superior by early Thursday. As it does so, rain will \ndevelop over the Upper Midwest. The western edge of this systems\ndeformation band will impact far southeastern ND into west \ncentral MN. The northwestern edge of the precipitation shield \nwill have a quite tight gradient in rainfall totals. This \nwestern edge looks to extend from near Gwinner to near Fargo to \nthe Red Lakes and Baudette. Small shifts in the systems track \nwill lead to locations along this line receiving little (a tenth\nor two) of rain or more significant values (half inch or so). \nLocations in west central MN, where the deformation band will \nhave a residence time of up to 24 hours, will see the highest \nrainfall totals. Rainfall totals of up to 1 inch (locally \nhigher) are forecasted in our southeastern tier of counties.",
